    "gold_solution": "```python\nimport math\nfrom gurobipy import *\n\ndef solve_problem():\n    m = Model(\"Facility_Location\")\n    m.setParam(\"TimeLimit\", 300)\n\n    # Problem Data\n    facilities = list(range(3))  # F0..F2\n    customers = list(range(14))  # C0..C13\n\n    # Facility coordinates (x, y)\n    facility_coords = [(14, 71), (78, 53), (28, 94)]\n\n    # Customer coordinates (x, y)\n    customer_coords = [(50, 62), (12, 0), (28, 31), (13, 60), (78, 42), (75, 59), (51, 10), (81, 0), (18, 86), (48, 5), (8, 82), (5, 70), (47, 10), (63, 42)]\n\n    facility_capacities = [235.0, 163.0, 267.0]\n    customer_demands = [31.0, 26.0, 50.0, 22.0, 47.0, 11.0, 47.0, 14.0, 13.0, 14.0, 38.0, 46.0, 20.0, 38.0]\n    fixed_costs = [433.0, 330.0, 408.0]\n    transport_rate = 0.5  # $/km/unit\n\n    # COMPUTE distance matrix from coordinates (INDUCED COSTS)\n    def euclidean_distance(coord1, coord2):\n        return math.sqrt((coord1[0] - coord2[0])**2 + (coord1[1] - coord2[1])**2)\n\n    distances = {}\n    for i in facilities:\n        for j in customers:\n            distances[(i, j)] = euclidean_distance(facility_coords[i], customer_coords[j])\n\n    # Decision Variables\n    # Open_F{i}: Binary - whether facility i is opened\n    open_facility = {}\n    for i in facilities:\n        open_facility[i] = m.addVar(vtype=GRB.BINARY, name=f\"Open_F{i}\")\n\n    # Ship_F{i}_C{j}: Continuous - units shipped from facility i to customer j\n    ship = {}\n    for i in facilities:\n        for j in customers:\n            ship[(i, j)] = m.addVar(lb=0.0, vtype=GRB.CONTINUOUS, name=f\"Ship_F{i}_C{j}\")\n\n    # Objective: Minimize fixed costs + transport costs\n    # Transport cost = distance * rate * units shipped\n    obj = quicksum(fixed_costs[i] * open_facility[i] for i in facilities)\n    obj += quicksum(\n        distances[(i, j)] * transport_rate * ship[(i, j)]\n        for i in facilities for j in customers\n    )\n    m.setObjective(obj, GRB.MINIMIZE)\n\n    # Demand satisfaction constraints\n    for j in customers:\n        m.addConstr(\n            quicksum(ship[(i, j)] for i in facilities) >= customer_demands[j],\n            name=f\"Demand_C{j}\"\n        )\n\n    # Capacity constraints (can only ship if facility is open)\n    for i in facilities:\n        m.addConstr(\n            quicksum(ship[(i, j)] for j in customers) <= facility_capacities[i] * open_facility[i],\n            name=f\"Capacity_F{i}\"\n        )\n\n    m.optimize()\n    return m\n```",
    "LLM_description": "Subject: Decision on Which Warehouses to Open and How to Serve Customers\n\nTeam,\n\nWe need to decide which of our three candidate warehouse sites to open and how to allocate shipments so that we meet demand across our customer base at the lowest overall cost. The candidate warehouse locations are provided in Facility Coordinates:\n  - F0: (14, 71)\n  - F1: (78, 53)\n  - F2: (28, 94), and the fourteen customer delivery points are listed in Customer Coordinates:\n  - C0: (50, 62)\n  - C1: (12, 0)\n  - C2: (28, 31)\n  - C3: (13, 60)\n  - C4: (78, 42)\n  - C5: (75, 59)\n  - C6: (51, 10)\n  - C7: (81, 0)\n  - C8: (18, 86)\n  - C9: (48, 5)\n  - C10: (8, 82)\n  - C11: (5, 70)\n  - C12: (47, 10)\n  - C13: (63, 42). Opening any warehouse will require a fixed outlay, summarized in [433.0, 330.0, 408.0]. Each site has a capacity limit that cannot be exceeded, as shown in [235.0, 163.0, 267.0], and each customer has a demand that must be fully satisfied, detailed in [31.0, 26.0, 50.0, 22.0, 47.0, 11.0, 47.0, 14.0, 13.0, 14.0, 38.0, 46.0, 20.0, 38.0].\n\nShipping costs will be driven by how far we move product and how much we ship. Our transport rate is 0.5. Distances between warehouses and customers are calculated as straight-line (Euclidean) distances from the map coordinates, so we will measure the direct point\u2011to\u2011point distance between each warehouse and each customer when estimating delivery costs.\n\nKey rules to keep in mind:\n- A warehouse can only ship product if we decide to open it.\n- Each customer\u2019s full demand must be met.\n- Each warehouse can ship up to, but not beyond, its capacity.\n- Shipping costs scale with both distance and shipment volume.\n\nOur objective is to minimize total cost, which is the combination of facility opening costs and the shipping costs required to serve all customers. The outcome we\u2019re looking for is a clear recommendation on which warehouse or warehouses to open and an assignment of customer volumes to those sites that achieves the lowest total cost while honoring capacity limits and fully satisfying demand.",
    "LLM_description_template": "Subject: Decision on Which Warehouses to Open and How to Serve Customers\n\nTeam,\n\nWe need to decide which of our three candidate warehouse sites to open and how to allocate shipments so that we meet demand across our customer base at the lowest overall cost. The candidate warehouse locations are provided in {FACILITY_COORDINATES}, and the fourteen customer delivery points are listed in {CUSTOMER_COORDINATES}. Opening any warehouse will require a fixed outlay, summarized in {FIXED_COSTS}. Each site has a capacity limit that cannot be exceeded, as shown in {FACILITY_CAPACITIES}, and each customer has a demand that must be fully satisfied, detailed in {CUSTOMER_DEMANDS}.\n\nShipping costs will be driven by how far we move product and how much we ship. Our transport rate is {TRANSPORT_RATE}. Distances between warehouses and customers are calculated as straight-line (Euclidean) distances from the map coordinates, so we will measure the direct point\u2011to\u2011point distance between each warehouse and each customer when estimating delivery costs.\n\nKey rules to keep in mind:\n- A warehouse can only ship product if we decide to open it.\n- Each customer\u2019s full demand must be met.\n- Each warehouse can ship up to, but not beyond, its capacity.\n- Shipping costs scale with both distance and shipment volume.\n\nOur objective is to minimize total cost, which is the combination of facility opening costs and the shipping costs required to serve all customers. The outcome we\u2019re looking for is a clear recommendation on which warehouse or warehouses to open and an assignment of customer volumes to those sites that achieves the lowest total cost while honoring capacity limits and fully satisfying demand."
}